Subject: Re: possible pkgsrc emacs framework improvements?
To: Thomas Klausner <wiz@NetBSD.org>

(DRAFT -- not sent.  The sweep line is still to be filled in.)

Hi Thomas,

Yes, I would like to work on this.  I have a prototype of all three
and have been building with it, so rather than say "sounds good" here
is what it does and what it turned out to cost.

  https://github.com/zakinko/pkgsrc-zakinko/tree/emacs-framework

I should say up front that I work in Japanese and am not strong in
English, so I write these with the help of a translation.  If
something reads oddly, or if I have misunderstood you, please tell me
plainly and I will rework it.

Version selection.  modules.mk now does what lang/python does:
EMACS_VERSION_REQD picks one version and refuses if the package cannot
use it, so a bulk pass leaves it alone instead of building it for
something else; otherwise EMACS_VERSION_DEFAULT (EMACS_TYPE keeps
working, it is just the default now); otherwise the first version the
package accepts.  _EMACS_VERSIONS_ALL is newest first, so "first
accepted" means the package's first choice, the way
PYTHON_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED is written.

Choosing from EMACS_VERSIONS_ACCEPTED alone turned out not to be safe.
A package can name a version that is no longer in the table, and
selecting it leaves _EMACS_PKGDIR empty, so the include dies with
"Cannot open /version.mk" -- a fatal error that stops the make rather
than failing the one package.  Nothing in pkgsrc is in that state
today; you took jde, leim21, bbdb2 and nxml-mode out along with
emacs21, and I checked that no package on trunk still names it.  But
my tree here is from 2 September and fifteen packages in it do, which
is what the window looks like each time a version is retired.  So the
choice is made from the intersection of what the package accepts and
what modules.mk still lists, and a version that exists is kept so the
includes resolve while PKG_FAIL_REASON says what happened:

  devel/apel, EMACS_VERSION_REQD=emacs21
    "pkgsrc no longer has emacs21"
    "pkgsrc has: emacs31 emacs31nox ... xemacs214nox"

  devel/apel, EMACS_VERSION_REQD=emacs20
    "This package does not build with emacs20"
    "Accepted versions are: emacs29 emacs29nox ... emacs31nox"

Those two have different causes and different places to look, so they
do not share a sentence.

Install location and name.  The lisp goes to
share/emacs/<version>/site-lisp, which Emacs already searches, and
EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX is ${_EMACS_TYPE}-, so pcl-cvs becomes
emacs20-pcl-cvs and apel becomes emacs29-apel, emacs29nox-apel,
emacs30-apel and so on, as you described.

pbulk.  Three lines in mk/pbulk/pbulk-index.mk, the same shape as the
python and ruby entries.

What it costs.  The figures below are from trunk, not from my tree: I
fetched the Makefile and PLIST of each package that reads modules.mk,
leaving out wip and the emacs and xemacs packages themselves, which is
87 packages.

The install location is nearly free.  Of the 86 with a PLIST, 77
already write ${EMACS_LISPPREFIX}; six write the path by hand and want
changing (cqual, rsltc, tamago-tsunagi, mailutils, twittering-mode,
emacs-dict-client).  Of the 26 that pass a lisp directory into their
own build, 25 already pass the variable; only chat/zenicb does not.

The name is where the work is, and most of it is not in modules.mk.
EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX only takes effect where the package spells it in
PKGNAME, and 22 of the 87 do not, so they still build as plain
dash-el, cqual, po-mode.  Adding it moves PKGBASE, and six variables
under mk/ take ${PKGBASE} as their default:

  PKGWILDCARD and PKG_SYSCONFVAR in bsd.pkg.mk, FILEBASE in
  pkgformat/pkg/package.mk, and GITHUB_PROJECT, GITLAB_PROJECT and
  CODEBERG_PROJECT in mk/fetch.

GITHUB_PROJECT is the one that bites: ten of the 87 take their
distfile from GitHub without setting it, so the fetch asks for
github.com/ziglang/emacs30nox-zig-mode and gets a 404, and WRKSRC
points where the archive does not unpack.  One line each fixes it.  No
package in this set uses gitlab or codeberg.

Nine packages spell ${PKGBASE} themselves, and that is a second way
for the same move to break them.  PKGBASE is in PLIST_SUBST, so a
package that uses it on both sides stays consistent; one that uses it
on one side does not.  I have looked at four so far: editors/gnuserv
lists share/doc/${PKGBASE} in INSTALLATION_DIRS while installing to
share/doc/gnuserv, so README ends up as a file of that name rather
than in a directory; editors/matlab-mode creates the directory as
${PKGBASE:C|^xemacs-||} and installs into ${PKGBASE}, which means it
has been broken under xemacs all along; misc/color-theme has
${PKGBASE} in MASTER_SITES, so its Savannah fetch moves too; and
www/emacs-w3m sets WRKSRC to ${WRKDIR}/${PKGBASE}-${GITHUB_TAG}.

Six lines in the tree name a renamed package without the prefix and
can then never be satisfied -- devel/flim adds an unprefixed
apel>=10.8 to BUILDLINK_API_DEPENDS.apel while apel's own
buildlink3.mk already says ${EMACS_PKGNAME_PREFIX}apel>=10.  Three of
the six are under wip.

Two of the things I had to fix were in my own modules.mk, and only
building showed them.  EMACSLOADPATH for the build still named the old
shared site-lisp, so anything byte-compiling against another elisp
package stopped with "Cannot open load file"; and the
EMACS_VERSION_REQD path had the same empty-_EMACS_PKGDIR fatal I
thought I had already fixed on the other branch.

The part that cannot be found by reading at all is a package whose
PLIST uses the variable but whose install writes the path itself.
devel/pcl-cvs is one: nothing in its Makefile says lispdir, and the
bundled makefile has lispdir = $(datadir)/emacs/site-lisp.  I tried to
find these with a filter over PLIST and Makefile and it gave both
misses and false alarms and called 62 packages suspect, so I dropped
it and built instead.

<<SWEEP RESULTS>>

